{"subjectId":"python","id":"loops","title":"Loops & Iteration Patterns","group":"Getting Started","order":7,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Python's <code>for</code> walks over <em>any iterable</em> — there is no C-style <code>for (i = 0; …)</code>. This topic collects the loop patterns, helpers and pitfalls you meet every day.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-numbers\"></i><code>range</code> in detail</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>print(list(range(5)))                 # [0, 1, 2, 3, 4]\nprint(list(range(2, 12, 3)))          # [2, 5, 8, 11]\nprint(list(range(5, 0, -1)))          # [5, 4, 3, 2, 1]\nprint(list(range(3, 0)))              # []   ← empty: a negative step is needed to count down\nr = range(0, 10**12, 2)               # no list is built — O(1) memory\nprint(len(r), r[10], r[-1])           # 500000000000 20 999999999998\nprint(999_999 in r, 1_000 in r)       # False True   ← O(1) arithmetic check</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Goal</th><th>Loop header</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0 … n−1</td><td><code>for i in range(n)</code></td></tr><tr><td>1 … n inclusive</td><td><code>for i in range(1, n + 1)</code></td></tr><tr><td>n−1 down to 0</td><td><code>for i in range(n - 1, -1, -1)</code> or <code>reversed(range(n))</code></td></tr><tr><td>Every other index</td><td><code>for i in range(0, n, 2)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Items and their index</td><td><code>for i, x in enumerate(xs)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Two lists in step</td><td><code>for a, b in zip(xs, ys)</code></td></tr><tr><td>Neighbouring pairs</td><td><code>for a, b in zip(xs, xs[1:])</code> or <code>itertools.pairwise(xs)</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-rotate-clockwise\"></i><code>while</code> loops and <code>while … else</code></h3>\n<p>Use <code>while</code> when you don't know the number of iterations in advance. Like <code>for</code>, it takes an <code>else</code> that runs only if the loop ended <strong>without</strong> <code>break</code>.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>n, steps = 27, 0\nwhile n != 1:                         # Collatz sequence\n    n = n // 2 if n % 2 == 0 else 3 * n + 1\n    steps += 1\nprint(steps)                          # 111\n\ndef is_prime(n):\n    if n &lt; 2:\n        return False\n    d = 2\n    while d * d &lt;= n:\n        if n % d == 0:\n            break                     # found a divisor\n        d += 1\n    else:\n        return True                   # no break → no divisor found\n    return False\n\nprint([k for k in range(20) if is_prime(k)])   # [2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, 19]</code></pre></figure>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>attempts = 0\nwhile True:                           # the do-while idiom: body runs at least once\n    attempts += 1\n    if attempts * attempts &gt; 20:\n        break\nprint(attempts)                       # 5</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-door-exit\"></i>Breaking out of nested loops</h3>\n<p><code>break</code> only leaves the innermost loop. Three ways out of two:</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>grid = [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6], [7, 8, 9]]\n\n# 1. A flag checked by the outer loop\nfound = None\nfor r, row in enumerate(grid):\n    for c, val in enumerate(row):\n        if val == 5:\n            found = (r, c)\n            break\n    if found:\n        break\nprint(found)                          # (1, 1)\n\n# 2. Put the loops in a function and return (usually the cleanest)\ndef find(grid, target):\n    for r, row in enumerate(grid):\n        for c, val in enumerate(row):\n            if val == target:\n                return r, c\n    return None\nprint(find(grid, 8), find(grid, 0))   # (2, 1) None\n\n# 3. One flat loop over all coordinates\nfrom itertools import product\nhit = next(((r, c) for r, c in product(range(3), range(3)) if grid[r][c] == 9), None)\nprint(hit)                            # (2, 2)</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-right-left\"></i>Iterating dicts, in order, in reverse, in parallel</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>prices = {\"tea\": 20, \"coffee\": 35, \"juice\": 50}\nprint([k for k in prices])                        # ['tea', 'coffee', 'juice']\nprint([f\"{k}={v}\" for k, v in prices.items()])    # ['tea=20', 'coffee=35', 'juice=50']\nprint(sorted(prices, key=prices.get, reverse=True))   # ['juice', 'coffee', 'tea']\n\nnames = [\"asha\", \"ravi\", \"kiran\"]\nscores = [91, 84, 77]\nprint(list(enumerate(names, start=1)))    # [(1, 'asha'), (2, 'ravi'), (3, 'kiran')]\nprint(dict(zip(names, scores)))           # {'asha': 91, 'ravi': 84, 'kiran': 77}\nprint(list(zip(names, [1, 2])))           # [('asha', 1), ('ravi', 2)]   ← stops at the shortest\ntry:\n    list(zip(names, [1, 2], strict=True)) # 3.10+: insist on equal lengths\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(e)                              # zip() argument 2 is shorter than argument 1\nprint(list(reversed(names)))              # ['kiran', 'ravi', 'asha']\n\npairs = [(\"a\", 1), (\"b\", 2)]\nletters, numbers = zip(*pairs)            # \"unzip\"\nprint(letters, numbers)                   # ('a', 'b') (1, 2)</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Don't modify a collection while looping over it</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>nums = [1, 2, 2, 3, 4]\nfor x in nums:\n    if x % 2 == 0:\n        nums.remove(x)        # shifts later items left → the loop skips one\nprint(nums)                   # [1, 2, 3]   ← a 2 survived!\n\nnums = [1, 2, 2, 3, 4]\nprint([x for x in nums if x % 2])       # [1, 3]   ← build a new list instead\n\nd = {\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}\ntry:\n    for k in d:\n        if d[k] == 1:\n            del d[k]\nexcept RuntimeError as e:\n    print(e)                  # dictionary changed size during iteration\nd = {k: v for k, v in d.items() if v != 1}\nprint(d)                      # {'b': 2}</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-filter\"></i>Build a new collection</h3><p>A comprehension that keeps what you want — the idiomatic fix.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-copy\"></i>Loop over a copy</h3><p><code>for x in nums[:]:</code> or <code>for k in list(d):</code> — then mutating the original is safe.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-back\"></i>Loop backwards by index</h3><p><code>for i in range(len(xs) - 1, -1, -1): del xs[i]</code> — deletions don't shift unvisited items.</p></div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-flag\"></i>Sentinel loops &amp; <code>iter(callable, sentinel)</code></h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import io\nfrom functools import partial\n\nstream = io.StringIO(\"alpha\\nbeta\\n\\ngamma\\n\")\nlines = []\nwhile (line := stream.readline().rstrip(\"\\n\")):   # stop at the first empty line\n    lines.append(line)\nprint(lines)                                      # ['alpha', 'beta']\n\nf = io.StringIO(\"abcdefgh\")\nchunks = list(iter(partial(f.read, 3), \"\"))       # call f.read(3) until it returns \"\"\nprint(chunks)                                     # ['abc', 'def', 'gh']</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>The two-argument <code>iter(func, sentinel)</code> calls <code>func()</code> repeatedly and stops when it returns <code>sentinel</code> — perfect for reading fixed-size blocks from files and sockets.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-variable\"></i>Loop variables &amp; scope</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>for i in range(3):\n    pass\nprint(i)                      # 2   ← the loop variable survives the loop\n\nsquares = [j * j for j in range(3)]\nprint(\"j\" in dir())           # False   ← comprehension variables don't leak</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>If the loop body never runs (empty iterable), the loop variable is never assigned — using it afterwards raises <code>NameError</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bolt\"></i>Faster loops</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Slower</th><th>Faster</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>total = 0</code> + <code>for x in xs: total += x</code></td><td><code>sum(xs)</code></td><td>Built-ins loop in C</td></tr><tr><td>Loop + <code>append</code></td><td>List comprehension</td><td>No repeated <code>.append</code> lookup and call</td></tr><tr><td><code>if x in big_list</code> inside a loop</td><td>Convert to a <code>set</code> once</td><td>O(1) instead of O(n) per check</td></tr><tr><td><code>for i in range(len(xs)): xs[i]</code></td><td><code>for x in xs</code></td><td>No indexing per step</td></tr><tr><td><code>s += piece</code> in a loop</td><td><code>''.join(pieces)</code></td><td>Avoids O(n²) copying</td></tr><tr><td><code>obj.method</code> looked up each time</td><td><code>m = obj.method</code> before the loop</td><td>One attribute lookup instead of n</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>When would you use for … else?</summary><div><p>For searches: the <code>else</code> runs only when the loop completed without <code>break</code>, i.e. when nothing was found. It replaces a separate <code>found = False</code> flag.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you iterate over two lists at the same time?</summary><div><p><code>for a, b in zip(xs, ys)</code>. <code>zip</code> stops at the shortest input; use <code>zip(..., strict=True)</code> (3.10+) to raise on a length mismatch, or <code>itertools.zip_longest</code> to pad the shorter one.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is removing items from a list while iterating over it a bug?</summary><div><p>The list iterator walks by index. Removing an item shifts the rest left, so the item right after the removed one is skipped. Iterate over a copy, iterate backwards, or build a new list with a comprehension.</p></div></details></section>"}